1. FEATURES
-----------
* App states: FOREGROUND, BACKGROUND and SUSPENDED for every process.
* State-aware scheduler: different time slices per state, SUSPENDED apps
  never run, and BACKGROUND apps are throttled on low battery.
* Simulated battery: drains automatically from 100% to 0%.
* Low-Memory Killer: kills SUSPENDED apps first, then BACKGROUND apps,
  oldest (least recently active) first. FOREGROUND apps are never killed.
* Demo programs: an interactive launcher dashboard and a memory stress test.


2. REQUIREMENTS
---------------
* A RISC-V GCC toolchain (riscv64-elf-gcc, or riscv64-linux-gnu-gcc) plus
  binutils.
* qemu-system-riscv64.

Arch Linux:
    sudo pacman -S riscv64-elf-gcc riscv64-elf-binutils qemu-system-riscv

Debian/Ubuntu:
    sudo apt install gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu qemu-system-misc

If the Makefile cannot find the compiler, pass the prefix explicitly:
    make qemu TOOLPREFIX=riscv64-elf-


3. BUILD AND RUN
----------------
    make clean
    make qemu            # build and boot (3 CPUs, 128 MB RAM)
    make qemu CPUS=1     # single CPU: best for scheduler/throttle tests

Leave QEMU with: Ctrl-A, release, then X.


4. HOW IT WORKS
---------------

4.1 App states (kernel/appstate.h, kernel/proc.h)
  Each struct proc has:
    app_state    FOREGROUND (0), BACKGROUND (1) or SUSPENDED (2)
    last_active  tick of the last time the app was FOREGROUND-active
    cpu_ticks    timer ticks this process has used
    slice_left   ticks left in the current time slice
  New processes start as FOREGROUND. setappstate_k() changes the state, and
  moving an app to FOREGROUND refreshes last_active.

4.2 Scheduler (kernel/proc.c: scheduler)
  * FOREGROUND apps get a slice of 10 ticks, BACKGROUND apps get 3.
  * SUSPENDED apps are skipped. The one exception is a SUSPENDED process
    that has been killed: it is allowed to run once so it can notice the
    kill and exit (otherwise it would stay stuck forever).
  * When battery < 20% and the throttle policy is on, BACKGROUND apps are
    only considered on every 3rd scheduler pass.
  * Every continue/break/return path releases p->lock; the scheduler and
    setappstate_k() were audited for this.

4.3 Battery (kernel/proc.c, kernel/trap.c)
  * clockintr() (CPU 0) counts timer ticks and calls battery_tick() every
    BATTERY_DRAIN_TICKS ticks.
  * battery_tick() lowers the global battery by 1, never below 0.
  * battery is protected by battery_lock; getbattery() and setbattery()
    use the same lock.

4.4 Low-Memory Killer (kernel/proc.c: lmk_check)
  lmk_check() is called from scheduler() with no locks held.
    1. It runs at most once per tick (kfreepages() walks the whole free
       list, so checking more often would be wasteful).
    2. After a kill it waits LMK_COOLDOWN_TICKS ticks, giving the victim
       time to exit and be reaped so memory is actually returned.
    3. If free pages >= LMK_MIN_FREE_PAGES, it does nothing.
    4. Otherwise it picks ONE victim among non-essential processes:
         - never init, never FOREGROUND, never one already killed
         - SUSPENDED before BACKGROUND
         - within the same class, smallest last_active (oldest) first
    5. The victim gets p->killed = 1 and is woken if sleeping. A SUSPENDED
       victim is moved to BACKGROUND so that it can be scheduled and exit.
    6. The kill is logged on the console:
         lmk: free pages low, killed pid N (name, STATE)
  last_active is updated on the timer interrupt only while the process is
  FOREGROUND, so it really means "when was this app last in use".

  Note: in xv6 a process's memory is freed when its parent calls wait().
  Apps must therefore have a parent that reaps them. The launcher starts
  apps so that init (which reaps forever) becomes their parent.


5. USER PROGRAMS
----------------
launcher   Interactive dashboard: battery bar, free memory, process table.
             run <prog> [args]   start an app (FOREGROUND)
             hog                 start memhog
             idle                start idleapp (set to BACKGROUND)
             fg|bg|sus <pid>     change an app's state
             kill <pid>          kill an app
             batt <0-100>        set the battery level
             <enter>             refresh the dashboard
             quit                leave the launcher
           Apps are started with a double fork so init reaps them.

memhog     Marks itself BACKGROUND (FOREGROUND apps are never killed), then
           allocates and touches memory in chunks until the LMK kills it.
             usage: memhog [chunk_kb]     (default 1024 KB)

idleapp    Sleeps forever holding a little memory. A harmless LMK victim.

schedtest, batterytest, testsubsys
           Tests for scheduling by state, low-battery throttling, and the
           meminfo/procinfo/battery system calls.


6. SYSTEM CALLS
---------------
    int setappstate(int pid, int state);   0 on success, -1 on error
    int getbattery(void);                  current level, 0-100
    int setbattery(int level);             0-100, -1 if out of range
    int setpolicy(int on);                 1 = throttle BG apps on low battery
    int meminfo(void);                     prints free/total pages
    int procinfo(void);                    prints the process table


7. CONFIGURATION (kernel/param.h)
---------------------------------
    LMK_MIN_FREE_PAGES    50    kill apps when free pages drop below this
    LMK_COOLDOWN_TICKS    2     minimum ticks between two LMK kills
    BATTERY_DRAIN_TICKS   500   timer ticks per 1% of battery drain

One timer tick is about 0.1 s, so the default drains 1% about every 50 s.
Use a small value (for example 10) to see the drain quickly in a demo.


8. TESTING AND DEMO
-------------------

Quick sanity check
    $ testsubsys

LMK kill order (the main demo)
    $ launcher
    launcher> idle
    launcher> idle
    launcher> idle
    launcher> sus <pid of one idleapp>
    launcher> hog
  Expected: the SUSPENDED idleapp is killed first, then the remaining
  BACKGROUND idleapps (oldest first), and memhog last if memory is still
  low. Press Enter to refresh and watch free memory recover.

Simple LMK test
    $ memhog
  memhog grows until: lmk: free pages low, killed pid N (memhog, BACKGROUND)

Killing a SUSPENDED app
    launcher> idle
    launcher> sus <pid>
    launcher> kill <pid>
  The pid must disappear from the table.

Low-battery throttling (use a single CPU)
    make qemu CPUS=1
    $ batterytest
  In phase 2 (battery 10%) the BACKGROUND worker should get far fewer ticks
  than the FOREGROUND worker.

Automatic battery drain
    $ launcher
  Refresh periodically (or lower BATTERY_DRAIN_TICKS) and watch the battery
  bar go down on its own.

Deadlock / regression check
    $ forktest
    $ usertests -q
  The second command should end with: ALL TESTS PASSED


9. SOURCE MAP
-------------
kernel/appstate.h   FOREGROUND / BACKGROUND / SUSPENDED constants
kernel/proc.h       struct proc, including the app-lifecycle fields
kernel/proc.c       scheduler, setappstate_k, battery_tick, lmk_check
kernel/trap.c       timer interrupt: cpu_ticks, last_active, time slices,
                    battery drain hook
kernel/sysproc.c    battery and app-state system calls
kernel/kalloc.c     kfreepages(), ktotalpages()
kernel/param.h      tunable constants
user/launcher.c     dashboard
user/memhog.c       memory stress test
user/idleapp.c      idle victim app
user/schedtest.c, user/batterytest.c, user/testsubsys.c   tests


10. KNOWN LIMITATIONS
---------------------
* The LMK kills one app per check, with a short cooldown, so under very
  fast allocation memory can briefly dip below the threshold.
* Memory is only returned once the killed app's parent reaps it.
* The battery is simulated; it does not model different apps draining at
  different rates.
* memhog allocates with sbrk(), which is eager. Lazy allocation (sbrklazy)
  would not consume memory until pages are touched.


